.TH tusqlite n 0.2 tclutils
.SH tclutils::tusqlite
.PP
Small, NULL\-safe helpers over an existing \fBsqlite3\fR database handle. The module does \fBnot\fR \fBpackage require sqlite3\fR itself — you pass it a handle you opened — so it loads anywhere, and only the calls that actually touch the database need the package present.
.PP
Why it exists: in \fBdb eval {INSERT ... VALUES(:x)}\fR the placeholder \fB:x\fR binds to the Tcl \fBvariable \fR\fBx\fR\fB in scope\fR, not to anything passed as an argument — a value you meant to pass as a dict silently becomes \fBNULL\fR. And an \fIomitted\fR bind variable yields SQL \fBNULL\fR, while a set\-but\-empty one yields \fB''\fR. \fBinsert\fR hides both traps.
.SS API
.CS
sqlite3 db mydata.db
::tclutils::tusqlite::insert db people {name Alice age 30}   ;# -> new rowid
::tclutils::tusqlite::insert db people {name Bob}            ;# age omitted -> NULL
::tclutils::tusqlite::insert db people \
    [list name Carol age [::tclutils::tusqlite::null]]       ;# explicit NULL
foreach row [::tclutils::tusqlite::rows db {SELECT name, age FROM people}] {
    puts [dict get $row name]
}
set n [::tclutils::tusqlite::value db {SELECT count(*) FROM people} -default 0]
.CE
.PP
Commands:
.IP \(bu
\fBinsert db table dict\fR — INSERT a row from a column→value dict; returns the new rowid. A missing key, or a value equal to \fB[null]\fR, is stored as SQL \fBNULL\fR; an empty string stays \fB''\fR. Identifiers are quoted, so reserved words and spaces in column names work.
.IP \(bu
\fBrows db sql ?params?\fR — run a query, return a list of column→value dicts (empty list if there are no rows).
.IP \(bu
\fBvalue db sql ?\-default d? ?\-params dict?\fR — the first column of the first row, or the default (\fB""\fR) when there is no row. The options keep a parameter dict apart from the default; the positional forms \fBvalue db sql default\fR and \fBvalue db sql default params\fR still work (0.2). Errors \fB{TCLUTILS TUSQLITE OPTION}\fR. Trap of the positional form: \fBvalue db $sql {id 1}\fR takes the dict as the default, not as parameters; the query runs without them, and with no row the dict comes back. Write \fBvalue db $sql \-params {id 1}\fR.
.IP \(bu
\fBupdate db table set where\fR — UPDATE the rows matching every key/value of \fBwhere\fR with the columns in \fBset\fR; returns the number of changed rows.
.IP \(bu
\fBdelete db table where\fR — DELETE the matching rows; returns their number.
.PP
\fBparams\fR is a dict that binds \fB:name\fR placeholders; caller variables are not visible to the query. A value equal to \fB[null]\fR binds SQL \fBNULL\fR. In \fBwhere\fR, \fB[null]\fR becomes \fBIS NULL\fR. An empty \fBwhere\fR is refused, so a typo cannot change or delete every row.
.CS
set r [::tclutils::tusqlite::rows db {SELECT * FROM people WHERE age > :a} {a 20}]
::tclutils::tusqlite::update db people {age 31} {name Alice}
::tclutils::tusqlite::delete db people {name Bob}
.CE
.IP \(bu
\fBquoteId name\fR — quote an SQL identifier (wrap in double quotes, doubling any embedded ones).
.IP \(bu
\fBnull\fR — the sentinel value that \fBinsert\fR maps to SQL \fBNULL\fR.
.PP
Errors: an empty insert or set dict → \fB{TCLUTILS TUSQLITE EMPTY}\fR; an empty where dict → \fB{TCLUTILS TUSQLITE WHERE}\fR; a bad parameter name → \fB{TCLUTILS TUSQLITE PARAM <name>}\fR. Verified on Tcl 8.6 (sqlite3 3.45); loads on 9.x (the database\-backed calls need an sqlite3 build for that version).
